  [3] cost
      exact 37.2 s   shell(R=8) 12.6 s   ratio 2.95x

==============================================================================
WHAT THIS SHOWS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.

  THE RESULT IS THE REFINEMENT BEHAVIOUR, not any single error.  The
  shell route SATURATES -- its ratio per doubling falls toward 1.0,
  which is a floor, because its truncation is scale-invariant: the
  cell and the shell shrink together.  The exact route does not, and
  approaches second order.  That is the difference between removing a
  bias and reducing a discretisation error.

  ⚠ AN EXPECTATION SET BEFORE RUNNING WAS WRONG, and the record is
  kept rather than quietly revised.  This script originally argued
  the two routes 'should come out close, since reach 8 is only mildly
  truncated'.  The reach sweep in settle_collocation_everywhere.py had
  ALREADY shown reach 8 is NOT nearly converged -- still falling as
  R^-1.5 with no saturation.  The premise was wrong, not the result.

  ⚠ AND THE MIXED CASE DIPS THROUGH ZERO at n_z = 4.  Its 3.9e-7 is a
  sign change, not an accuracy; a two-point comparison would have
  reported it as a 17x gain.  Three meshes were run for that reason.

  The durable claim is structural: the exact route has NO reach
  parameter, where the shell route provably cannot converge one
  (R ~ 80 needed, shape-dependence switching on at R ~ 50).
==============================================================================
